.TH FIND 1
.SH NAME
Find \- find a file by part of its name, in acme
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B Find
[
.I dir
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Find
opens an
.IR acme (1)
window,
.IB dir /+Find\f1,
that lists files.
Its first line is a query, and the lines after it are the files that match it,
best first, as many as a hundred, changing as the query is typed.
A file matches if the query's characters are in its name in order;
a match counts for more at the start of a name or a word, in a run, and in the file's own name
rather than its directories'.
A query with no upper-case letter ignores case.
.PP
A newline typed at the end of the query opens the first match;
button 3 or button 2 on a match opens that one.
The file is opened by the plumber's
.B edit
port, and the window goes.
.PP
The files are those of
.I dir
and below, as
.IR walk (1)
gives them, without
.BR .git ;
or, if
.I dir
is in a git repository and the
.B git9
package (9front's git) is added (see
.IR pkg (1)),
the repository's tracked files, as
.IR git (1)'s
.B git/walk
gives them.
With no
.IR dir ,
.I Find
takes the directory of the file in acme window
.B $acmewin
(the focused window, as
.I acmestart
sets it) or
.BR $winid ,
or else the current directory.
.SH EXAMPLES
.I Acmestart
binds Command-P to
.IR Find ,
in
.BR /lib/hotkey/acme :
.IP
.EX
cmd-p	{test -e /mnt/acme/index || mount $acmesrv /mnt/acme; Find}
.EE
.SH SOURCE
plan9web's
.B src/cmd/Find.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR acme (1),
.IR hotkey (1),
.IR pkg (1),
.IR plumb (1),
.IR walk (1)
